Q: JaCoCo indicates missed branch on two-part RegEx matches/find which is proved to be covered between multiple unit tests A: <p>The problem is that when the first if statement is true, it covers the second false-branch of the third if statement, which means the test can never cover all the execution branches. Because each if contains two operands, there are two ways for each if to be false. Jacoco expects that you test each one of them.</p>
<pre><code>        if (matcher.matches() &amp;&amp; uuidMatcher.find())
            System.out.println(&quot;Satisfied (1): matcher.matches &amp; uuidMatcher.find&quot;);
        else if (!matcher.matches() &amp;&amp; uuidMatcher.find())
            System.out.println(&quot;Satisfied (2): !matcher.matches &amp; uuidMatcher.find&quot;);
        else if (matcher.matches() &amp;&amp; !uuidMatcher.find())
            // If matcher.maches() == true and uuidMatcher.find() == true,
            // execution took the first if and never came here. Jacoco
            // complains because you never test the second false-branch
            // of this if-statement. You only test the case where
            // matcher.matches() == false &amp;&amp; uuidMatcher.find() == false.
            // Solution is to remove uuidMatcher from this if because it
            // is dead code.
            System.out.println(&quot;Satisfied (3): matcher.matches &amp; !uuidMatcher.find&quot;);
        else
            System.out.println(&quot;Satisfied (4): !matcher.matches &amp; !uuidMatcher.find&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>But then it's better to reformat the code to make the if branches easier to follow:</p>
<pre><code>        if (matcher.matches()) {
            if (uuidMatcher.find()) {
                System.out.println(&quot;Satisfied (1): matcher.matches &amp; uuidMatcher.find&quot;);
            } else {
                System.out.println(&quot;Satisfied (3): matcher.matches &amp; !uuidMatcher.find&quot;);
            }
        } else {
            if (uuidMatcher.find()) {
                System.out.println(&quot;Satisfied (2): !matcher.matches &amp; uuidMatcher.find&quot;);
            } else {
                System.out.println(&quot;Satisfied (4): !matcher.matches &amp; !uuidMatcher.find&quot;);
            }
        }
</code></pre>
